{
  "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
  "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:00 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Colleen R. Sinzdak",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under the plain text of the INA, an alien has the burden of establishing eligibility for asylum and withholding of removal. An alien may sometimes meet that burden through his credible testimony but only when, among other things, the testimony is persuasive and outweighs other evidence of record."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice BREYER: Well, I -- I -- my question here is -- reflects my concern with the legal mind. The legal mind loves to make distinctions, but sometimes that should be resisted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. SINZDAK",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You should not consider it. The alien did not raise the -- the hospital records in his briefing before the Board, and, under 1252, administrative exhaustion is required. It's a prerequisite for judicial review."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE ALITO",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "what should we make of the hospital records? Are they something that we should not consider at all?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Okay. Let's suppose you're right about that just for the moment. What -- what difference does that make if a court of -- of -- you know, a court of appeals has to review for substantial evidence and reasonableness. Doesn't it have to also inquire as to whether the Board reasonably treated the presumption?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. SINZDAK",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I don't think the statute requires the Board to make an explicit credibility determination. The only thing the statute says in that regard is that, if the fact-finder or if -- if it had not made an explicit adverse credibility determination, the Board should apply the presumption. It doesn't then say --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Yes, Mr. Katyal, the -- I'm a bit confused. The -- you say that there's no magic word requirement. But what if there is just the -- the -- the Board makes a choice or the IJ makes a choice between two sets of evidence? Isn't it implicit that it found one more credible than the other?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Good morning. I -- I think you and every -- everyone seems to agree with this: The Ninth Circuit's wrong when they say that you, even though where there's no finding, you assume that the person is credible. That isn't what the statute said."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: I don't know that I have very much to add here. The -- the Ninth Circuit said, and I'm quoting from the opinion in your case, \"We have repeatedly held that where the BIA does not make an explicit adverse credibility finding, the court must assume that the petitioner's factual contentions are true.\" I understand you now to acknowledge that that was an inaccurate statement of the law. Isn't that right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. Katyal, this seems like a -- this -- this is a pretty simple case, right? There's -- there's basically two pieces of evidence in it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the government doesn't even make that argument, and I think as your hypothetical demonstrates why, because there is no language anywhere in there that we don't believe criminals or anything like that, or we tend to believe probation officers or the like."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Katyal (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So isn't the surrounding circumstances, and then you look at this case, and, basically, it just comes back -- down to do you believe Mr. Alcaraz or do you believe the probation report, doesn't it -- isn't it just clear that the Board believed the probation report rather than Mr. Alcaraz? And, if that's so, isn't it clear that, essentially, the Board decided to rebut the presumption?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAGAN",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But aren't you just asking, like -- I mean, it seems as though"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "82c675ca70732877c0f5133b3a6a073b3ae20dd0e79e965fcf4111e0c05272bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-82c675ca7073-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 84,
        "start": 84
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: There's a difference -- maybe there's a difference between silence and -- and -- and a requirement of -- as an explicit statement. Must they make an explicit statement? Are they under the same obligation as the IJ?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1155",
